" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1ef0c83c8902997fa341dc96539ef5f6a8de5da387b543a9eb0ba664d8b9b82"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{ }x"] ["math" "\\text{ }x+{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] ["math" "\\text{ }x+Δx"] ["math" "x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{ }y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] ["math" "\\text{ }y+{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] ["math" "\\text{ }y+{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] ["math" "y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{ }\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] ["math" "\\text{ }y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] ["math" "\\text{ }{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] ["math" "y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331162" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y+3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-y+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}-{y}_{1}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}-{y}_{2}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{17}{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}-{y}_{3}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-\\frac{17}{6}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{8}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{13}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{16}{6}+\\frac{28}{6}+\\frac{30}{6}+\\frac{13}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{87}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ | $\\frac{29}{12}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{29}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ | $\\frac{29}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\,\\frac{11}{4}\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(1)≈\\frac{11}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1ef0c83c8902997fa341dc96539ef5f6a8de5da387b543a9eb0ba664d8b9b82"} {"question" "q-331213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{29}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-331213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-8y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-8y \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =4{x}_{1}-8{y}_{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{17}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =4{x}_{2}-8{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-8\\cdot \\frac{17}{16} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{4},\\,1+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =4{x}_{3}-8{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-8\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-4+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot (-4)+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(-6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{5}{4})≈\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1ef0c83c8902997fa341dc96539ef5f6a8de5da387b543a9eb0ba664d8b9b82"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy