workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-339819" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-353417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\cdot (x+1),\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with one step to approximate $y(0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-353417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table and add the initial condition data.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x+1) \\\\  & =1(-1+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2},1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & ={y}_{1}({x}_{1}+1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={y}_{2}({x}_{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\ =(-1+1,\\,1+\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot 1) \\\\ =(0,\\frac{13}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & ={y}_{3}({x}_{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}\\cdot (0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{13}{8})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{31}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{31}{48}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{31}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{31}{48}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{79}{48}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈\\frac{79}{48}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-353417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1ef0c83c8902997fa341dc96539ef5f6a8de5da387b543a9eb0ba664d8b9b82"} {"question" "q-353417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056247/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6e2ee02cc250cc199c87073f67f201052383d2d68467039d25171b62e7ad585"} {"question" "q-353417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{13}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{41}{56}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{79}{48}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/140